4. THE ATTEMPT-3 FIX ITSELF -- PASS (the central item, verified hardest)

4a. The regression test genuinely fails without the fix.
Scratch copy b2-nofix, single edit, loader.go:1080 reverted to the attempt-2
form `if pair.Left.Hash == decision.SuppressedHash {`:

    $ go test ./internal/services/dataloader/ \
        -run TestApplyDuplicateDetection_SuppressEarlierInFile_KeptIsLeft -v
    === RUN   TestApplyDuplicateDetection_SuppressEarlierInFile_KeptIsLeft
        stable_id_test.go:586: resolved Left = "2b0d013a1d6966cf" ("Lucid"),
            want the KEPT check "78aa352ca55d57de"
        stable_id_test.go:590: resolved Right = "78aa352ca55d57de"
            ("Check #996583"), want the SUPPRESSED bill pay "2b0d013a1d6966cf"
    --- FAIL: TestApplyDuplicateDetection_SuppressEarlierInFile_KeptIsLeft
    FAIL    budget2/internal/services/dataloader     0.003s

The roles come back exactly inverted -- the suppressed bill pay reported as
Left/kept -- which is the attempt-2 user-visible defect verbatim. With the fix
in place, in the real tree:

    --- PASS: TestApplyDuplicateDetection_SuppressEarlierInFile_KeptIsLeft
    ok      budget2/internal/services/dataloader     0.003s

4b. The LoadData-before-SaveDuplicateDecision ordering is present AND
load-bearing. stable_id_test.go:534 calls loader.LoadData() (asserting 2 rows
and that ts.Transactions[0] is the "Lucid" bill pay), then :552 takes
loader.UnresolvedDuplicates() and asserts pair.Left.Hash == billPay.Hash, and
only then :565 calls SaveDuplicateDecision with SuppressedHash = billPay.Hash
(a legacy content hash, as the /duplicates form renders). The failure output
above is itself the proof the ordering matters: the test SAVED
SuppressedHash == pair.Left.Hash, yet the reverted `pair.Left.Hash ==
decision.SuppressedHash` evaluated FALSE. The only mechanism that can make
those unequal is canonicalKey rekeying the posted hash to the row's StableID
(duplicate_decisions.go:107-108), which requires the stableIDIndex that only
a prior LoadData publishes. So the test demonstrably exercises the real
rekeying path, not a no-op.

4c. Both stored key forms are handled. stable_id.go:41-49:

    func identityMatchesSuppressed(t models.Transaction, key string) bool {
        if key == "" { return false }
        if t.StableID != "" && key == t.StableID { return true }
        return key == t.Hash
    }

StableID branch = the post-A1 normal case (proved by 4a/4b). Legacy-hash
branch = a pre-A1 decision or a row outside the loaded set where canonicalKey
is a no-op; it is the same comparison the old code did, so that path is
strictly preserved, and TestApplyDuplicateDetection_LegacyPairKeyStillResolves
(stable_id_test.go:362) covers the legacy shape. The empty-key guard prevents
a kept_both / malformed decision with SuppressedHash=="" from matching a row
whose Hash is "". StableID and Hash namespaces are disjoint (StableIDs contain
'|', hashes are 16 hex chars), so neither branch can cross-match.

