=====================================================================
FAILING FINDING -- attempt 3's third change, internal/services/mcpsvc/admin/
resolve.go:26, is factually false and describes a value the code cannot produce
=====================================================================

The change adds this jsonschema description to resolveOutput.SuppressedHash --
the OUTPUT of the resolve_duplicates MCP tool, read by a model:

  "the stored identity of the suppressed side for kept_winner;
   SaveDuplicateDecision rekeys the posted hash to the row's StableID, so this
   may be a StableID rather than the legacy content hash list_duplicates
   reports -- it is the value actually persisted in duplicate_decisions.json"

Both of its claims are wrong, and the second is not merely usually wrong but
impossible:

(a) The returned value is never the persisted value. resolve.go:126-147 builds
    a LOCAL `decision`, resolve.go:155 passes it to
    SaveDuplicateDecision(pairKey string, decision DuplicateDecision) -- by
    VALUE (duplicate_decisions.go:89) -- so the rekeying at
    duplicate_decisions.go:107-108 mutates the callee's copy only. resolve.go:162
    then reports the caller's untouched local: the posted legacy hash.

(b) The returned value can never be a StableID at all. resolve.go:139-145
    rejects any kept/suppressed input that is not equal to pair.Left.Hash or
    pair.Right.Hash, and those are legacy content hashes. So the field is
    always a legacy hash, and the sentence "this may be a StableID" describes
    an outcome the validation forbids.

Proved by execution, not inspection. Scratch copy b2-probe, one added test file
in the admin package using the existing newLiveDeps/connect harness:

    posted suppressed_hash   = "9f58fcc457d8ab90"
    reported out.Suppressed  = "9f58fcc457d8ab90"
    on-disk duplicate_decisions.json:
    {
      "decisions": {
        "ebf6c2daa009e909": {
          "kept_hash": "file:checking.csv|2024-02-03|-25000|0",
          "suppressed_hash": "file:checking.csv|2024-02-06|-25000|0",
          "outcome": "kept_winner",
          "decided_at": "2026-08-17T17:25:18.536051635Z"
        }
      }
    }
    MISMATCH: reported suppressed_hash "9f58fcc457d8ab90" is NOT the value
    persisted on disk

Why this blocks acceptance rather than being a note:

 - It is one of attempt 3's three deliverables, introduced in a file listed in
   A1.3.files, and it is the piece added specifically to address the
   StableID-vs-hash confusion that caused the attempt-2 rejection. The
   direction of the confusion was inverted, so the same misunderstanding has
   simply moved from behavior into the machine-facing contract.
 - It is asymmetrically worse than doing nothing: before attempt 3 the field
   carried no description (uninformative); now it carries a false one that an
   LLM client will act on -- e.g. reporting to the user which identity the
   decision was stored under, or expecting to find that string in the file.
 - It is invisible to the suite. No test asserts anything about the content of
   resolveOutput.SuppressedHash for kept_winner (resolve_test.go only asserts
   it is EMPTY for kept_both, at :126-127 and undo_test.go:85-86), which is
   the same "green tests over a wrong observable" shape as ruling 2026-08-16a.

Remedy is one line, either direction: correct the description to say the field
echoes the legacy hash the caller posted (which list_duplicates reports), or
change resolve.go:162 to report the value actually persisted. If the latter,
it needs a test, and the description then also needs to survive the input
validation at :139-145.

Nothing else in attempt 3 is in doubt. The regression fix at loader.go:1080 /
stable_id.go:41-49 and its test are correct, and I verified them by reverting
the fix outside the repo and watching the roles invert.

